corrnet: compute the CTC loss without cuDNN

Why: criterion_calculation passes CPU int32 targets, so on this stack
(PyTorch 2.11 / NGC 26.02, cuDNN 9.19) nn.CTCLoss takes cuDNN's CTC kernel
even when the two sequences of a batch differ in length. PyTorch 1.x used
cuDNN only when every input length equalled T, so upstream originally ran
the native kernel for such batches. On real batches the cuDNN loss value is
right but its gradient w.r.t. the logits is wrong whenever lengths differ:
cosine similarity to a float64 CPU reference 0.06-0.96, max abs error up to
2.65 against a reference max of 0.39; equal-length batches and the native
kernel match to ~1e-5 (ctc_check.py --arm compare-grads, runs
ctc-diagnostics-{vac,corrnet}-phoenix-001, preflight-{smkd,tlp}-gsl-002,
preflight-tlp-phoenix-002). Under AMP, GradScaler then skipped 26-50 of 60
updates (7-8 without cuDNN). Disabling cuDNN for the CTC call alone restores
the native computation; nothing else changes. Same change as
papers/yu-2025-cvsign/patches/corrnet-ctc-without-cudnn.patch.
Applies to: corrnet @ a812814f4deefda0b75ab1dcc90b106c62912263 (git apply from the repo root)
---
diff --git a/slr_network.py b/slr_network.py
index 9d49a0d..db14c05 100644
--- a/slr_network.py
+++ b/slr_network.py
@@ -129,6 +129,12 @@ class SLRModel(nn.Module):
         return loss
 
     def criterion_init(self):
-        self.loss['CTCLoss'] = torch.nn.CTCLoss(reduction='none', zero_infinity=False)
+        ctc_loss = torch.nn.CTCLoss(reduction='none', zero_infinity=False)
+
+        def ctc_loss_without_cudnn(*args):
+            with torch.backends.cudnn.flags(enabled=False):
+                return ctc_loss(*args)
+
+        self.loss['CTCLoss'] = ctc_loss_without_cudnn
         self.loss['distillation'] = SeqKD(T=8)
         return self.loss
